"Near-equality of the Penrose Inequality for rotationally symmetric Riemannian manifolds (https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.2165)\nciteturn4academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.1 years ago; This article is the sequel to our previous paper [LS] dealing with the near-equality case of the Positive Mass Theorem.We study the near-equality case of the Penrose Inequality for the class of complete asymptotically flat rotationally symmetric Riemannian manifolds with nonnegative scalar curvature whose boundaries are outermost minimal hypersurfaces.\nTitle: Near-equality of the Penrose Inequality for rotationally symmetric Riemannian manifolds\nAuthors: Dan A. Lee, Christina Sormani\nDate: Fri Sep  9 21:56:23 2011\n\nThis article is the sequel to our previous paper [LS] dealing with the near-equality case of the Positive Mass Theorem. We study the near-equality case of the Penrose Inequality for the class of complete asymptotically flat rotationally symmetric Riemannian manifolds with nonnegative scalar curvature whose boundaries are outermost minimal hypersurfaces. Specifically, we prove that if the Penrose Inequality is sufficiently close to being an equality on one of these manifolds, then it must be close to a Schwarzschild space with an appended cylinder, in the sense of Lipschitz Distance. Since the Lipschitz Distance bounds the Intrinsic Flat Distance on compact sets, we also obtain a result for Intrinsic Flat Distance, which is a more appropriate distance for more general near-equality results, as discussed in [LS]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLipschitz inextendibility of weak null singularities from curvature blow-up (https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.18838)\nciteturn4academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Title: Lipschitz inextendibility of weak null singularities from curvature blow-up\nTitle: Lipschitz inextendibility of weak null singularities from curvature blow-up\nAuthors: Jan Sbierski\nDate: Fri Sep 27 15:34:51 2024\n\nWe prove the $C^{0,1}_{\\mathrm{loc}}$-inextendibility of weak null singularities without any symmetry assumptions. The proof introduces a new strategy to infer $C^{0,1}_{\\mathrm{loc}}$-inextendibility from the blow-up of curvature. The assumed blow-up is expected to be satisfied for weak null singularities in the interior of generic rotating black holes. Thus, we expect the result presented here to directly contribute to the resolution of the $C^{0,1}_{\\mathrm{loc}}$-formulation of the strong cosmic censorship conjecture in a neighbourhood of subextremal Kerr.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSharp conditioning and quantitative stability of optimal transport (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07577)\nciteturn4academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 days ago; Title: Sharp conditioning and quantitative stability of optimal transportAuthors: Yuanlong Ruan\nTitle: Sharp conditioning and quantitative stability of optimal transport\nAuthors: Yuanlong Ruan\nDate: Tue Oct  6 01:12:35 2026\n\nWhen a feasible plan is obtained whose quadratic transport cost is known to be close to the optimal cost, we try to determine how close the feasible plan is to the true optimal map under mild density and moment conditions. The source and target may be unbounded or have non-compact supports. We show that for a source with density in $L^p$ and an $n$-th moment, let $s=1-1/p$ and $η=sn/[n+(d-1)s]$. Under the target constraint $\\int |y|^m[\\log(e+|y|)]^β\\,dν\\leqslant1$, the worst-case class-uniform conditioning has a sharp modulus comparable to \\[ t^{η(m-2)/[m(1+η)-η]} [\\log(e/t)]^{-β(2+η)/[m(1+η)-η]}, \\] whenever $t>0$ is small. This holds for $m\\geqslant2$ and $β\\geqslant0$. When $m=2$, every $β>0$ gives a sharp logarithmic modulus, whereas the $m=2,\\,β=0$ extreme has no vanishing modulus. The matching lower bound is verified for both the squared map error and barycentric projection error. The sharp map error bounds allow us to directly derive quantitative stabilities of common source Brenier maps without passing through potential estimates, thereby avoiding loss of information. These stabilities strictly improve the corresponding results of Delalande-Merigot \\cite{delalande2023quantitative} and Letrouit-Mérigot \\cite{letrouit2026gluing} under identical or weaker settings, particularly no convexity of the source support or bounds of the source density are assumed.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGluing methods for quantitative stability of optimal transport maps (https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04908)\nciteturn4academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Title: Gluing methods for quantitative stability of optimal transport maps ... We establish quantitative stability bounds for the quadratic optimal transport map $T_\\mu$ between a fixed probability density $\\rho$ and a probability measure $\\mu$ on $\\mathbb{R}^d$.\nTitle: Gluing methods for quantitative stability of optimal transport maps\nAuthors: Cyril Letrouit, Quentin Mérigot\nDate: Thu Nov  7 17:48:49 2024\n\nWe establish quantitative stability bounds for the quadratic optimal transport map $T_\\mu$ between a fixed probability density $\\rho$ and a probability measure $\\mu$ on $\\mathbb{R}^d$. Under general assumptions on $\\rho$, we prove that the map $\\mu\\mapsto T_\\mu$ is bi-H\\\"older continuous, with dimension-free H\\\"older exponents. The linearized optimal transport metric $W_{2,\\rho}(\\mu,\\nu)=\\|T_\\mu-T_\\nu\\|_{L^2(\\rho)}$ is therefore bi-H\\\"older equivalent to the $2$-Wasserstein distance, which justifies its use in applications. We show this property in the following cases: (i) for any log-concave density $\\rho$ with full support in $\\mathbb{R}^d$, and any log-bounded perturbation thereof; (ii) for $\\rho$ bounded away from $0$ and $+\\infty$ on a John domain (e.g., on a bounded Lipschitz domain), while the only previously known result of this type assumed convexity of the domain; (iii) for some important families of probability densities on bounded domains which decay or blow-up polynomially near the boundary. Concerning the sharpness of point (ii), we also provide examples of non-John domains for which the Brenier potentials do not satisfy any H\\\"older stability estimate. Our proofs rely on local variance inequalities for the Brenier potentials in small convex subsets of the support of $\\rho$, which are glued together to deduce a global variance inequality. This gluing argument is based on two different strategies of independent interest: one of them leverages the properties of the Whitney decomposition in bounded domains, the other one relies on spectral graph theory.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGluing methods for quantitative stability of optimal... (https://arxivlens.com/explained/gluing-methods-for-quantitative-stability-of-optimal-transport-maps-5218-2615669)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Gluing methods for quantitative stability of optimal transport maps ...   * Stability of transport maps is achievable without depending on the space dimension: small changes in the target distribution lead to controlled, predictable changes in the transport map. ...   * Two constructive tools enrich the approach: Whitney decompositions and spectral graph theory give complementary ways to glue local estimates into a global bound.\n\n# Gluing methods for quantitative stability of optimal transport maps\n\nCyril Letrouit, Quentin Mérigot\n\n10 views 6 citations View full paper →\n\nImagine you have a bunch of grains of sand sprinkled on a table in a certain shape, and you want to slide them into a new shape avoiding big gaps or stretches. This paper shows that when you change the target shape a little bit, the way the sand moves (the map that guides the move) changes in a controlled, predictable way. It’s like saying small changes in the target keep the steps you take smooth and not wildly different.\n\n## Explain Like I'm 5\n\nImagine you have a bunch of grains of sand sprinkled on a table in a certain shape, and you want to slide them into a new shape avoiding big gaps or stretches. This paper shows that when you change the target shape a little bit, the way the sand moves (the map that guides the move) changes in a controlled, predictable way. It’s like saying small changes in the target keep the steps you take smooth and not wildly different.\n\n## Detailed Explanation\n\nThis research is about how to measure and control the way mass is rearranged from one shape (given by a fixed density ρ) to another (a probability measure μ) in space. The core idea comes from optimal transport, where you find the most efficient “map” Tμ that pushes ρ to μ. The authors focus on the quadratic cost case, which leads to a special kind of map called the Brenier map. They study how sensitive this map is to changes in μ, asking: if we nudge μ a bit, how much does Tμ change? They prove that the dependence is bi-Hölder, meaning small changes in μ lead to predictably small changes in Tμ, and vice versa, with exponents that don’t depend on the dimension of the space. This gives a robust, dimension-free way to compare nearby transport plans using a linearized distance W2,ρ(μ,ν) defined by the L2 difference between their transport maps. The paper confirms this stability in several practical scenarios: 1) when ρ is log-concave with support everywhere and we perturb it a bit, 2) when ρ stays nicely bounded away from 0 and ∞ inside a John domain (a broad class that includes nice shapes like bounded Lipschitz domains), and 3) for some common densities on bounded domains that behave nicely near the boundary. They also show that if the domain is not a John domain, you can fail to get any Hölder stability, so the geometry of the space matters. To prove these results, they develop a technique that looks locally at how much the Brenier potential (a function guiding the transport) can vary in small convex pieces of the domain, and then glues these local pieces together to get a global statement. Two complementary methods help with the gluing: one uses a Whitney-type decomposition that breaks the area into well-behaved chunks, the other uses ideas from spectral graph theory to connect the pieces. These fresh, constructive proofs not only yield the stability results but also deepen the toolbox for studying how optimal transport behaves under perturbations.\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n  * Stability of transport maps is achievable without depending on the space dimension: small changes in the target distribution lead to controlled, predictable changes in the transport map.\n  * A linearized, practical distance W2,ρ constructed from the difference of transport maps provides a faithful stand-in for the usual Wasserstein distance when comparing nearby distributions.\n  * The geometry and regularity of the domain matter: stability holds in broad, natural settings (like John domains with bounded density) but can fail on more irregular domains.\n  * Local-to-global strategy is powerful: by analyzing how the Brenier potential varies in small convex pieces and then stitching these pieces together, one can derive strong global stability results.\n  * Two constructive tools enrich the approach: Whitney decompositions and spectral graph theory give complementary ways to glue local estimates into a global bound.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Lipschitz inextendibility of weak null singularities from curvature blow-up (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/397502002_Lipschitz_inextendibility_of_weak_null_singularities_from_curvature_blow-up)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; hole interiors of [7], supplemented by a curvature blow-up assumption, are locally ... Lipschitz inextendibility of weak n